.SS Macro expansion functions
These are functions for explicitly expanding macros in an expression.
If an optional environmant is not included then only the default
built\-in macros will be expanded.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](macroexpand\-1 expr {{environment}})\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
If the top\-level of \f[CR]expr\f[R] is a macro call then one round of
expansion will be done, otherwise \f[CR]expr\f[R] will be returned.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](macroexpand expr {{environment}})\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
If the top\-level of \f[CR]expr\f[R] is a macro call then that macro
will be repeatedly expanded until it is no longer a macro call,
otherwise \f[CR]expr\f[R] will be returned.
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB](macroexpand\-all expr {{environment}})\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Returns the expansion from the expression \f[CR]expr\f[R] where all
macro calls have been expanded with \f[CR]macroexpand\f[R].
.SS Evaluate expressions
\f[B]\f[CB](eval expr {{envionment}})\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
Evaluate the expression \f[CR]expr\f[R].
Note that only the pre\-defined lisp functions, erlang BIFs and exported
functions can be called.
Also no local variables can be accessed.
To access local variables the expr to be evaluated can be wrapped in a
\f[CR]let\f[R] defining these.
.PP
For example if the data we wish to evaluate is in the variable
\f[CR]expr\f[R] and it assumes there is a local variable \f[CR]foo\f[R]
which it needs to access then we could evaluate it by calling:
.IP
.EX
(eval \(ga(let ((foo ,foo)) ,expr))
.EE
